DISSIPATIVE AND CONSERVING SYSTEMS A dynamical system is dissipative, if its phase space volume contracts continuously, viz when ∇ ·  ̇x < 0 for all x(t). The system is said to be conserving if the phase space volume is a constant of motion, viz if ∇ ·  ̇x ≡ 0.
Mechanical systems, i.e. systems described by Hamiltonian mechanics, are conserving in above sense. One denotes this result from classical mechanics as “Liouville’s theorem”.